          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get anxious most days and it interferes with work.",
          "answer": "I'm sorry you're dealing with this. I can't tell you whether what you're describing is a particular condition, but I can share what the reference material says.\n\nThe general clinical reference on generalized anxiety disorder describes persistent, excessive worry about a range of everyday matters, occurring more days than not, that is hard to control and causes problems with functioning. It also notes that worry is often accompanied by restlessness, fatigue, trouble concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and poor sleep (condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder). Those are the kinds of things a clinician would ask you about, so it may help to note which apply to you and how long they have lasted.\n\nThe physical activity material also says that regular activity can help with anxiety, and that starting small and keeping it up tends to work better than an ambitious plan that gets dropped (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity).\n\nA clinician such as a doctor or mental health professional is the right person to talk to about this. Your work is being affected, so it is reasonable to book an appointment soon rather than wait. If your anxiety becomes severe, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, contact emergency services or a crisis line straight away.\n\nThe search results did not cover treatment options, so I can't tell you what helps beyond activity, and I can't advise on medication.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
